sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch03-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because register machines idealize programs in low-level languages such as C, Basic, Java or assembler, searching many millions of short programs in such languages typically yields at least a few with complex, seemingly random behavior.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Short programs in ordinary low-level languages can behave randomly";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 3, “The World of Simple Programs”; section “Symbolic Systems” (from p. 102), p. 102. Scope: short programs in practical low-level programming languages"
}
